    "result_text": "All tests pass (BUILD SUCCESS, 16 tests, 0 failures). The change is complete.\n\n## Summary\n\nTelephone numbers are now stored and returned in E.164 form, with duplicate detection comparing E.164 values.\n\n**`OwnerRestControllerV1.java`** \u2014 replaced the old \"strip to 10 digits\" normalization with a `normalizeTelephone` helper that:\n- strips spaces, dashes, and brackets;\n- keeps a leading `+` and its country code when present;\n- otherwise assumes country code `+61` and drops a single leading `0` from the national digits;\n- requires 8\u201315 digits after the `+`, throwing `InvalidRequestException` (\u2192 400) when it can't form valid E.164.\n\nThe resulting E.164 string is stored via `setTelephone(...)` and used for the duplicate lookup (`findOwnerByTelephone`), so `0412 345 678` and `+61 412 345 678` collide (\u2192 409).\n\n**`Owner.java`** \u2014 updated the entity's Bean Validation constraint from `^[0-9]{10}$` (plus `@Digits`) to `^\\+[0-9]{8,15}$` so the persisted E.164 value passes JPA validation on save.\n\nVerified against the checkpoint tests:\n- `0412 345 678` \u2192 `+61412345678` (national \u2192 E.164)\n- `+64 21 123 456` \u2192 `+6421123456` (explicit country code preserved)\n- `12` \u2192 400 (cannot form valid E.164)\n- national/international forms of the same number \u2192 409 duplicate\n\nThe `telephone` DB column is `VARCHAR(20)`, which comfortably fits E.164 (max 16 chars), so no schema change was needed.",
